# Readability & Big-Picture Audit (CS / AI papers)

**Role:** Clarity reviewer and **readiness gate** for already-written draft prose. In agent pipelines this guide is a **reviewer** whose output feeds a **rewriter**; in standalone use it is a human-facing audit report.

**Trigger:** User asks to score readability (易读性 / 易读程度), check clarity, or check whether each section/subsection opens with an accessible big picture before diving into detail. Also runs when **global-prose-readability-gate** or another parent guide embeds this audit.

**Scope (non-goals):** This guide judges **prose readability and signposting only**. It does **not** judge scientific novelty, experimental rigor, or venue fit — that is `academic-review-paper`. In **standalone** mode it audits and reports; it does not rewrite unless the user asks. In **embedded** mode (via **global-prose-readability-gate**), revise the draft until the pass bar is met.

**Standard is single-sourced.** The bar for a "big picture" is defined in `academic-writing-paper` §4 (Open with the big picture). The **de-AI vocabulary checklist** below is mirrored from `academic-writing-paper` §1 and §7 so agent pipelines can audit without chasing another file; add new entries in `academic-writing-paper` §7 first, then sync here.

**Output language:** Match the user's request; default **English**, but report in Chinese if the paper or request is Chinese. Audit Chinese and English text with the same criteria.

**Input:** A pasted draft, or a file path (e.g. `docs/papers/outline/s04-methodology.md`). When given a path, read the file(s) first, then segment by Markdown headings before auditing.

**Priority:** For readability-audit tasks, this guide overrides conflicting guidance elsewhere in the workspace.

**Invocation modes:**
- **Standalone — user** — a person asks for a readability score or audit; return the **human report** (below).
- **Standalone — pipeline** — the request includes an explicit machine-audit marker (`[AUDIT_FOR: REWRITER]`, `readiness feedback for rewriter`, or similar); return the **machine report** only.
- **Embedded** — parent writing guide calls **global-prose-readability-gate**; apply this procedure internally, use the **machine report** for the revision loop, and deliver revised prose plus a brief gate summary to the user unless they asked for the full audit.

---

## Caller routing — pick one output shape

Before auditing, classify the **caller** and emit exactly one report shape:

| Caller | How to detect | Output |
|--------|---------------|--------|
| **Human** | Standalone user request with no pipeline marker | Human report |
| **Agent pipeline** | Embedded via **global-prose-readability-gate** or any parent writing guide; OR standalone with `[AUDIT_FOR: REWRITER]` / equivalent | Machine report |

When unsure, default to **human** if the user is clearly reading the audit; default to **machine** if the audit is an internal gate before rewrite.

Both shapes share the same audit logic (Part B first, then Part A) and the same **decision threshold** (below). They differ only in formatting and tone: humans get tables and illustrative suggested prose; agents get tagged defect logs and imperative rewrite directives.

---

## Decision threshold (routing signal)

Every audit — human or machine — must open with exactly one line:

`[DECISION: REWORK]` or `[DECISION: PASS]`

**REWORK** when **any** of these hold:
- Any Part A dimension rates **❌ Weak** (score 1–2)
- Any Part B content unit verdict is **⚠️ Partial** or **❌ Missing**

**PASS** only when all Part A dimensions are **✅ Strong** or **⚠️ Borderline** (3–5) **and** every Part B unit is **✅ Has big picture**.

The decision line is the **only** boolean gate downstream agents need. Do not ask a rewriter to infer pass/fail from a numeric score alone.

**Readiness score (NN/100)** remains a diagnostic rollup for humans and logging. Map the six Part A dimensions to NN/100 and a band — **90–100 Excellent · 75–89 Good · 60–74 Needs work · <60 Hard to read** — with a one-sentence verdict naming the single biggest readability cost. The score does **not** override the decision threshold above.

---

## What this guide produces

Two audit parts, in this order:

1. **Part A — Readability score:** can a *relatively junior researcher* follow the prose on one read?
2. **Part B — Big-picture signpost audit:** does every new content unit open with a plain overview of the problem, the why, and the how?

The **audience anchor** for both: a *first-year grad student in an adjacent subfield, or a reviewer from a neighboring area* — competent, but with no prior context on this specific work. "Readable" means readable to that person, not to the authors.

---

## Part A — Readability scoring

Rate each dimension **✅ Strong / ⚠️ Borderline / ❌ Weak** with a **1–5** number and a one-line reason grounded in a quoted example from the text.

| # | Dimension | Strong (5) looks like |
|---|-----------|-----------------------|
| 1 | **Sentence load** | One idea per sentence; mixed lengths; few sentences over ~30 words; claim+mechanism+number+implication not bundled into one line. |
| 2 | **Entry points** | Each section/subsection opens with an accessible academic big picture before the formalism (this is Part B; carry its result here). |
| 3 | **Term hygiene** | Each acronym/symbol defined on first use; operations named with the established field word (*bind*, not *recover*); no inflated synonym from the de-AI checklist (below); shallow layer uses common academic words, not casual prose. |
| 4 | **Transitions** | Causal/relational links (because, therefore, however), not empty sequencers ("Next…", "现在…") or "Moreover/Furthermore" on every paragraph. |
| 5 | **Notation accessibility** | Symbols introduced in words before the equation; the reader meets an idea in prose before its compressed form. |
| 6 | **Paragraph shape** | Default claim → mechanism → evidence → limit; the unit does not open on its most compressed sentence. |

